BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Circles of Sound - Meditative concert
DTSTART:20260614T200000
DTEND:20260614T000000
UID:5825043@oslo-events-calendar
DESCRIPTION:Welcome to a magical experience at the Emanuel Vigeland Museum
 \, with its incredible acoustics and atmosphere.\n\n  Gongs are unique ins
 truments\, rich in overtones. They can create a sense of being in a timele
 ss state. The gong can be deeply relaxing and provide a beautiful\, medita
 tive experience for listeners. Several different gongs are used\, with a l
 arge symphonic gong as the main instrument. During the gong bath\, the bea
 utiful overtones from the gongs will be interwoven with tones from instrum
 ents such as flute\, harp\, guitar\, vocals\, hang drum\, didgeridoo\, and
  Tibetan singing bowls.\n\n  Circles of Sound is known for mythical tones\
 , Nordic primordial songs\, and meditative music. It will be a powerful an
 d beautiful experience!\n\n  To experience the full potential of the gongs
 \, the audience is recommended to lie down during the concert\, although i
 t’s also possible to sit. The gongs are played in sequences\, guiding yo
 u through gong sequences and musical landscapes created by Circles of Soun
 d.\n\n  More about Circles of Sound: Circles of Sound consists of Grethe M
 idgaard Torrissen and Frans Hamer.\n\n  Grethe is a gong player and singer
  who has\, over many years\, allowed her interest in meditation and nature
  to unfold in musical landscapes. Her Sámi roots have also inspired parts
  of the musical picture.\n\n  Frans Hamer is a multi-instrumentalist and m
 usic therapist from the Netherlands. For many years\, he has worked in hea
 lthcare in the Netherlands\, providing music for all stages of life\, from
  childhood to life’s end. Impressively\, he can switch between instrumen
 ts like flute\, guitar\, harp\, hang drum\, didgeridoo\, and piano\, with 
 a unique ability to touch the essence of life and the meditative through t
 he music he composes and plays.\n\n  More about the Emanuel Vigeland Museu
 m: The Emanuel Vigeland Museum in Slemdal is one of Oslo’s best-kept sec
 rets. The beautiful\, dimly lit barrel-vaulted room has unique acoustics w
 ith approximately 20 seconds of reverberation. The walls and ceiling are e
 ntirely covered with frescoes by Emanuel Vigeland\, and several of his scu
 lptures also adorn the room. The mural Vita depicts human life from conce
 ption to death—dramatic and erotic. Large bronze figure groups echo the 
 walls’ tribute to the mystery of procreation. Entering the museum is an 
 experience in itself.\n\n  Practical information: There is limited space f
 or 30 people. Experience shows that it is advisable to reserve a place in 
 advance\, as these concerts usually sell out. NB! Bring warm clothing and 
 a comfortable mat\, warm blanket/sleeping bag. It is relatively cold in th
 e Emanuel Vigeland Museum. Doors open 30 minutes before the event.
LOCATION:Emanuel Vigeland Museum - Oslo
URL:https://www.visitoslo.com/en/whats-on/events/?tlp=5825043&name=Circles
 -of-Sound--Meditative-concert
END:VEVENT
